<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ercole Consalvi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1757">1757</a>–<a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Cardinal Consalvi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ercole Consalvi was an Italian cardinal and cardinal secretary of state to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii">Pope Pius VII</a>. He negotiated the Concordat of 1801 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815, recovered almost all the Papal States for the pope. He then reformed their administration and laws on modern lines, though many of his reforms were reversed after his retirement in 1823.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ercole-consalvi.jpg" alt="Ercole Consalvi" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Lawrence · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Lawrence_(1769-1830)_-_Ercole,_Cardinal_Consalvi_(1757-1824)_-_RCIN_404940_-_Royal_Collection.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ercole Consalvi was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on 8 June 1757, the son of Mario Giuseppe Consalvi, Marquess of Toscanella, and Countess Claudia Carandini of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a>. The family descended from the Brunacci of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>; his grandfather had taken the Consalvi name to inherit a fortune. After his father's death in 1763 he was placed in the care of Cardinal Andrea Negroni. He studied with the Piarists and at the seminary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frascati">Frascati</a> founded by Cardinal Henry Benedict Stuart, who helped him to high office in the Roman Curia, and he took doctorates in civil and canon law at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapienza-university-of-rome">La Sapienza</a> in 1789.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the French Revolutionary Army invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1798, Consalvi was jailed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-santangelo">Castel Sant'Angelo</a>, but he was soon released and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vi">Pope Pius VI</a> in exile. He was secretary of the conclave in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> that elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii">Pope Pius VII</a>, who made him cardinal-deacon and secretary of state in August 1800. He introduced free trade in the Papal States, withdrew depreciated money and admitted many laymen to government offices. He was ordained subdeacon and deacon in December 1801, but never priest or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>.</p><p>His opposition to the Papal States joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-system">Continental System</a> forced his resignation in June 1806. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> annexed the Papal States in 1809 he was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, refused a pension of 30,000 francs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, and in 1810 was one of thirteen black cardinals stripped of property and status for refusing to attend Napoleon's marriage to Archduchess Marie Louise. Confined at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a>, he rejoined the pope in 1813 and persuaded him to retract the concessions of the Concordat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fontainebleau">Fontainebleau</a>. Exiled again to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beziers">Béziers</a>, he was freed on 2 April 1814 and reappointed secretary of state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As papal plenipotentiary he set out a policy of papal neutrality, first stated at the Neapolitan revolt of 1821. For the rest of Pius VII's pontificate he was the virtual ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, concluding another concordat with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1817 and helping to re-establish the English College in 1818. A highly cultivated man devoted to music and archaeology, he laid out promenades along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>, restored ancient monuments and filled the museums with statues from excavations. He secured the Protestant sculptor Thorvaldsen's commission for Pius VII's tomb in St Peter's. He died on 24 January 1824 and is buried in San Marcello al Corso.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ercole Consalvi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1800" aria-label="Rome on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ercole Consalvi"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5788 3947h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5788 3947h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.89,12.48&amp;year=1800">What was here in 1800? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
